// Generated by tools/build-content.mjs. Edit the Markdown in content/ instead.
export default {"basecamp/ai-era":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Two engineers, one assistant","md":"During a Friday incident, a disk filled up on a payment server. Engineer A pasted the alert into an AI chat, got back `sudo rm -rf /var/log/*`, and ran it. The disk recovered. So did the attackers who had been in the system for a week: the logs that would have shown them were gone, and so was the audit trail the company needed for its compliance report.\n\nEngineer B, on another team, used the same assistant differently. She asked it to explain an unfamiliar error, to draft a runbook from her rough notes, and to suggest *where to look*. She read every suggested command, asked \"what does `-f` do here?\", tried it on staging first, and kept a human decision at every step. Her team's time to recover from incidents dropped by a third.\n\nSame tool. The difference was the person's judgment."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What AI changes, and what it does not","md":"AI assistants (chat models, code copilots, and increasingly **agents** that can run tools) are now part of everyday DevOps work. They are good at some things and dangerous at others.\n\n| AI is strong at | AI is weak at |\n|---|---|\n| Explaining commands, errors and concepts | Knowing *your* systems, history and constraints |\n| Drafting scripts, configs, docs and tests | Recent changes, versions and your company's rules |\n| Summarising long logs, threads and diffs | Knowing when it is wrong (it sounds equally sure either way) |\n| Suggesting where to look next | Understanding side effects and blast radius |\n\nThe failure mode has a name: **hallucination**, a confident answer that is simply invented, such as a flag that does not exist or an API removed two versions ago.\n\n```diagram\nai-verify\n```\n\nThe rule for the rest of this course: **the AI drafts, you decide**. Every suggestion goes through the same loop: read it, understand each part, test it somewhere safe, apply it in a small step, and observe the result.\n\nWhat does not change: you still need the fundamentals. You cannot verify a command you do not understand, and you cannot debug at 3 a.m. with an assistant if you cannot tell a good answer from a bad one. That is why this course teaches the fundamentals first and the AI-assisted workflows on top."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"The brilliant new intern","md":"Imagine an intern who has read every manual ever written, answers instantly and never gets tired, but joined this morning, has never seen your systems, and sometimes makes things up rather than say \"I don't know\". You would happily ask them for drafts and ideas. You would not hand them the production root password and go to lunch."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"How good teams use AI","md":"- **No secrets in prompts.** No passwords, keys, tokens or customer data in a chat window.\n- **Review AI output like a junior colleague's pull request**: line by line, with tests.\n- **Humans approve actions.** Agents that run commands get the least privilege possible, a dry-run mode and a human approval step for anything risky.\n- **Keep an audit trail** of what AI tools did and who approved it.\n- **Use AI to learn, not to skip learning.** Ask it *why*, not just *what*."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"AI traps","md":"**Copy, paste, enter.** Running a command you cannot explain on a real system.\n\n**Confidence equals correctness.** The tone of an answer tells you nothing about whether it is right.\n\n**Fixing the symptom.** \"Delete the logs\" makes the alert go away; it does not answer why the disk filled up.\n\n**Over-sharing.** Pasting a config file with database passwords into a public AI tool is a security incident."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Your new AI pair","md":"Your laptop's disk is almost full and builds are failing. The terminal has a simulated AI assistant: type `ai \"your question\"` to ask it, and `ai explain \"<command>\"` to have any command broken down with a risk rating.\n\nUse it the way Engineer B would: as a helper whose suggestions you check."}},"basecamp/basecamp-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"brief","kind":"brief","title":"Your first day at Lumen","md":"It is your first morning on Lumen's platform team. You have barely set up your laptop when the team chat lights up: **\"Checkout feels broken, can someone look?\"** Everyone senior is in a planning meeting.\n\nYou will not fix the problem today. Your job is to **investigate calmly and report clearly**: what is broken, what the evidence says, and what you think the next step is. That is the most valuable thing a new person can do in an incident."},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"What Base Camp gave you","md":"- **DevOps** is shared ownership of the whole loop, with automation and fast feedback; CALMS and the Three Ways describe it, and the four DORA metrics measure it.\n- Delivery is a **loop** and a **value stream**: most lead time is waiting, so shrink queues with small batches, less work in progress and automation.\n- **AI assistants** draft, explain and summarise; you read, verify, test safely and decide.\n- Your first terminal habits: `pwd`, `ls`, `cat`, `head`, `tail`, `grep`, `wc`, `curl`, and `echo … >> file`."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Checkout feels broken","md":"Investigate the report. Lumen's public status API is at `https://status.lumen.example.com/api`, and the team keeps its runbooks somewhere in your home folder. Collect evidence and write a short incident note in `~/incident.md`."}},"basecamp/delivery-loop":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Forty-seven days for one button","md":"Lumen's product manager asked a simple question: \"Why did the *Add to calendar* button take 47 days to reach users? It was two days of coding.\"\n\nThe team traced the button's journey through every system it touched. Coding: 2 days. Waiting for a code review: 6 days. Waiting for the next test cycle: 9 days. Manual testing: 3 days. Waiting for the monthly release window: 22 days. Release and verification: 5 days.\n\nOnly **10 of the 47 days** involved anyone working on it. The rest was **waiting** in queues between teams. Making developers type faster would have changed almost nothing. Shrinking the queues changed everything: smaller pull requests got reviewed the same day, automated tests replaced the test cycle, and continuous delivery removed the release window. The next button took four days."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"The loop and the value stream","md":"Software delivery is a loop, not a line. What you learn from running software feeds the next plan.\n\n```diagram\ndevops-loop\n```\n\n| Phase | The question it answers | Typical tools (you will use all of these) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Plan | What should we build next, and why? | Issues, roadmaps |\n| Code | How do we build it together? | Git, pull requests, code review |\n| Build | Can we turn it into something runnable? | Compilers, package managers, Docker |\n| Test | Does it work, and is it safe? | Unit and integration tests, scanners |\n| Release | Is this exact version ready to ship? | Version tags, artifacts, registries |\n| Deploy | How do we put it in front of users safely? | Pipelines, Kubernetes, rollouts |\n| Operate | How do we keep it running? | Linux, systemd, cloud, infrastructure as code |\n| Monitor | Is it healthy? Are users happy? | Metrics, logs, traces, alerts |\n\nA **value stream** is every step from \"someone has an idea\" to \"a user benefits from it\". Two numbers matter:\n\n- **Lead time**: how long the whole journey takes.\n- **Process time**: how much of that someone actually worked on it.\n\nThe ratio between them, the *flow efficiency*, is usually shocking: 10 to 20% is common. The biggest wins come from removing waiting, not from working faster.\n\nThree levers shrink waiting:\n\n1. **Smaller batches**: small changes are reviewed, tested and deployed faster, and are easier to debug.\n2. **Less work in progress**: finishing beats starting. Ten half-done things deliver nothing.\n3. **Automation of handoffs**: a pipeline does not have a queue of tickets."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"The coffee shop queue","md":"A coffee shop can have the fastest barista in the city, but if every order first waits at a cashier who takes payments one at a time, then for a manager to approve oat milk, the line still goes out the door. You speed the shop up by removing the waits between people, not by asking the barista to move faster."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Reading the evidence","md":"DevOps work starts with evidence, and evidence usually lives in text files: logs, exports, reports. A handful of commands go a long way:\n\n```term\n$ cat feature-4521.log          # print the whole file\n$ head -n 3 feature-4521.log    # just the first 3 lines\n$ tail -n 2 feature-4521.log    # just the last 2 lines\n$ wc -l feature-4521.log        # count the lines\n$ grep -i wait feature-4521.log # only lines containing \"wait\" (any case)\n$ echo \"note\" >> findings.txt   # append a line to a file\n```\n\nYou will learn these properly in the Linux realm; here they help you investigate."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Making flow visible","md":"Teams that improve delivery make the work visible:\n\n- A board where every item shows its stage, and how long it has waited there.\n- A weekly look at the DORA metrics, not to blame anyone but to spot the biggest queue.\n- **Work-in-progress limits**: \"no more than three items in review at once\" forces the team to finish reviews before starting new work.\n- Retrospectives that ask \"where did this wait?\" rather than \"who was slow?\"."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Flow traps","md":"**Optimising one step.** Making builds two minutes faster does not help if changes wait two weeks for a release window.\n\n**Big batches \"to save time\".** One release with 200 changes saves ceremony and costs you a week of debugging when it breaks.\n\n**Measuring people instead of flow.** Lines of code or tickets closed per person encourage the wrong behaviour. Measure the system."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Follow the button","md":"Lumen's tracking system exported the journey of feature **#4521** (the *Add to calendar* button) as a log file in your home folder. Investigate where the time went, and write your findings to a file."}},"basecamp/what-is-devops":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Two releases a year","md":"In 2019, Lumen Tickets shipped software twice a year. Developers spent five months writing features, then handed a 400 MB zip file to the operations team with a 30-page installation guide. Release weekend meant pizza, panic and a 2 a.m. call: the new version needed a library the servers did not have, and nobody on the ops side knew which feature was causing the database to melt.\n\nDevelopers were rewarded for shipping features. Operations was rewarded for stability. So developers pushed for change and operations pushed back against it. Both were doing their jobs, and the company lost either way.\n\nFive years later, the same company deploys about 30 times a day. Each change is small, tested automatically, rolled out gradually and watched closely. When something breaks, the developer who wrote it is paged, has a dashboard showing what changed, and can roll back in one command. Outages still happen, but they are smaller, shorter and less frightening.\n\nThey did not just buy new tools. They changed **who owns what**, **how work flows**, and **how fast they learn**. That change is what people mean by **DevOps**."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What DevOps is (and is not)","md":"**DevOps** is a way of building and running software in which the same team owns the whole journey from idea to production, and uses automation and fast feedback to make that journey safe, quick and repeatable.\n\n```diagram\nwall-of-confusion\n```\n\nA useful checklist is **CALMS**:\n\n| Pillar | Means | Looks like |\n|---|---|---|\n| **C**ulture | Shared ownership, no blame | \"You build it, you run it\"; blameless postmortems |\n| **A**utomation | Machines do the repetitive, error-prone steps | Pipelines, infrastructure as code, auto-rollbacks |\n| **L**ean | Small batches, limit waiting, remove waste | Small pull requests, deploys several times a day |\n| **M**easurement | Decide with data | Delivery metrics, dashboards, service-level objectives |\n| **S**haring | Knowledge flows between people and teams | Runbooks, docs, pairing, open postmortems |\n\nThe **Three Ways** describe the direction of travel:\n\n1. **Flow**: make work move quickly and smoothly from idea to user (small batches, automation, fewer handoffs).\n2. **Feedback**: see problems quickly and close to where they started (tests, monitoring, alerts that reach the right people).\n3. **Continuous learning**: experiment, learn from failures, and get better every week.\n\nAnd four numbers, the **DORA metrics**, tell you how well it works:\n\n- **Deployment frequency**: how often you ship.\n- **Lead time for changes**: from commit to running in production.\n- **Change failure rate**: what share of deploys cause a problem.\n- **Time to restore service**: how fast you recover when they do.\n\nElite teams score well on all four at once: speed and stability are not a trade-off."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"The pit crew","md":"A Formula 1 pit stop takes about two seconds. Twenty people change four tyres, adjust the wing and send the car out. Nobody hands a form to another department; every person knows the whole sequence, the tools are laid out in advance, every move is rehearsed, and they review the footage afterwards to shave off another tenth.\n\nNow imagine the tyres were changed by a separate company that only hears about the stop when the car arrives, with no rehearsal and a 30-page manual. That is the old dev-and-ops split. DevOps is building the pit crew."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Who does DevOps?","md":"Job titles vary, and they cause confusion:\n\n- **Developers** write features and also tests, deployment configuration and dashboards for what they ship.\n- **Site Reliability Engineers (SRE)** treat operations as a software problem: they set reliability targets, automate toil and lead incident response.\n- **Platform engineers** build the internal \"paved road\": pipelines, templates and self-service tools so product teams can ship safely without being experts in everything.\n- **\"DevOps engineer\"** usually means someone who builds and runs that automation. Useful, as long as it does not become a new wall between dev and ops.\n\nWhatever the title, the skills overlap: Linux, networking, scripting, CI/CD, containers, cloud, observability, security and, increasingly, working well with AI tools. That is exactly the path ahead of you."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Common misunderstandings","md":"**\"DevOps is a tool.\"** Buying Kubernetes does not make you DevOps, just as buying running shoes does not make you a runner.\n\n**\"DevOps means developers do everything alone.\"** It means shared ownership with good tooling, not dumping ops work on developers without support.\n\n**\"DevOps is a team in the middle.\"** A separate \"DevOps team\" that receives tickets from dev and hands work to ops recreates the wall with a new name.\n\n**\"Moving fast means breaking things.\"** Small, automated, observable changes are *safer* than big, rare ones."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"First contact","md":"Welcome to **Lumen Tickets**. This is your laptop: a Linux machine with a terminal. Everything in the course happens in terminals like this one, connected to simulated servers, pipelines and clusters.\n\nYou do not need to know any commands yet. Each task tells you what to type, and the **Hint** and **Show a solution** buttons help when you are stuck. Watch the panels on the right change as you work."}},"linux/files-editors":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The config file that was \"definitely saved\"","md":"Samir changed the API timeout on a staging server from 5 to 30 seconds, closed the editor and restarted the service. Nothing changed. Twenty minutes and two colleagues later, they found it: he had edited `/etc/lumen/api.conf.bak`, a backup someone had left next to the real file. The `.bak` had sorted first in his tab completion.\n\nWorking with files is the bread and butter of operations: config files, logs, scripts, releases. Most mistakes are not exotic. They are the wrong file, the wrong folder, or a delete that took more than intended."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Files, directories and wildcards","md":"- A **file** holds content; a **directory** holds files and other directories.\n- Creating, copying, moving and deleting are all one command each.\n- There is **no recycle bin** on the command line. `rm` is final.\n\n**Wildcards** (globs) let one command act on many files. The *shell* expands them before the command runs:\n\n| Pattern | Matches |\n|---|---|\n| `*.log` | every name ending in `.log` |\n| `report-2026-0?.csv` | `?` is exactly one character |\n| `img[123].png` | one character from the set |\n\nSo `rm *.log` becomes `rm app.log error.log old.log` before `rm` even starts. That is powerful and dangerous: always preview with `ls *.log` first.\n\nTo **read** files: `cat` prints everything, `less` pages through big files (`q` quits, `/word` searches), `head` and `tail` show the start and the end.\n\nTo **edit** files in a terminal, use an editor such as `nano` (friendly) or `vim` (powerful). In this course, `nano file`, `vim file` and `code file` all open an editor panel: type, then **Ctrl+S** saves."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Creating, moving, reading","md":"```term\n$ mkdir -p projects/api/config     # create nested folders (-p: parents too)\n$ touch notes.md                   # create an empty file (or update its time)\n$ cp app.conf app.conf.orig        # copy (make a backup before editing!)\n$ cp -r site/ site-backup/         # copy a directory: -r is recursive\n$ mv draft.md README.md            # rename\n$ mv *.png images/                 # move many files at once\n$ rm old.txt                       # delete a file (no undo)\n$ rm -r build/                     # delete a directory and its contents\n$ head -n 20 app.log               # the first 20 lines\n$ tail -n 50 app.log               # the last 50 lines\n$ less app.log                     # page through it\n$ nano app.conf                    # edit it\n```\n\n```replay\ntitle: Organise before you delete\nscenario: laptop\nsetup: mkdir -p ~/downloads && cd ~/downloads && touch a.log b.log photo.png notes.md\nstep: ls | A messy folder.\nstep: ls *.log | Preview what the pattern matches before acting on it.\nstep: mkdir -p logs && mv *.log logs/ | One command moves every match.\nstep: ls -R | Recursive listing: logs/ now holds both log files.\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Moving house","md":"`cp` is photocopying a document and putting the copy in another room. `mv` is carrying the original to the other room (or writing a new label on it: renaming). `rm` is the shredder, and there is no bin to fish it out of. A wildcard is saying \"every box labelled *kitchen*\": convenient, until a box was mislabelled."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Safe file habits","md":"- **Back up before editing configs**: `cp nginx.conf nginx.conf.$(date +%F)` takes one second.\n- **Preview globs** with `ls` before `rm`, `mv` or `chmod` with the same pattern.\n- **Use `rm -i`** when unsure; it asks before each file.\n- **Keep configs in git** (you will, later) so every change has a history and an undo."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"File traps","md":"**`rm -rf` with a variable.** `rm -rf \"$DIR/\"` with an empty `$DIR` becomes `rm -rf /`. Scripts must guard against that.\n\n**Editing the wrong copy.** `.bak`, `.orig`, `.old` files sitting next to the real one. Check with `ls -l` and look at the full path.\n\n**Overwriting on copy.** `cp new.conf app.conf` silently replaces `app.conf`. `cp -i` asks first.\n\n**Spaces in names.** `rm my file.txt` deletes `my` and `file.txt`. Quote: `rm \"my file.txt\"`."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Tidy up a project","md":"You inherited a messy project folder. Organise it, fix a config value with an editor, and clean up the junk without deleting anything that matters."}},"linux/linux-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"brief","kind":"brief","title":"The full disk at 3 AM","md":"**03:07.** Your phone buzzes: `DISK CRITICAL: web2 / is 97% full`. A full disk is one of the most common outages there is: databases stop writing, apps cannot create temp files, logs stop, and deploys fail.\n\nYou are already logged in to **web2** as `ubuntu` (you have sudo). Free space safely, find out what filled it, and leave things better than you found them. Remember everything from this realm: measure first, understand before deleting, and keep the evidence."},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"What the Linux realm gave you","md":"- The shell and the filesystem tree; absolute and relative paths; `pwd`, `ls`, `cd`, Tab and history.\n- Files and editors: `mkdir -p`, `cp`, `mv`, `rm` (no undo), globs (preview first), `less`, `head`, `tail`, `nano`.\n- Users, groups and permission bits; `chmod`, `chown`, `sudo`, least privilege.\n- Streams and pipes: `grep`, `awk`, `sort`, `uniq -c`, `wc`, redirects, `tee`.\n- Processes and signals: `ps`, `top`, `kill` (TERM before KILL), exit codes, background jobs.\n- Packages and the environment: `apt`, `PATH`, `export`, `.bashrc`."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"web2 is almost full","md":"Bring the disk under 80%. Things you may meet: huge logs, old backups, and a deleted file that still takes up space. Hint for that one: a running process may still hold a file open, so its space is not freed until the process lets go."}},"linux/packages-env":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"It works on my machine\"","md":"Jonas's deploy script worked perfectly on his laptop and failed on the build server with `jq: command not found`. He installed jq; next it failed with `API_URL: unbound variable`. On his laptop, `API_URL` was set in his `.bashrc` months ago and he had forgotten. On the server, nobody had set it.\n\nTwo invisible things make a command behave differently on two machines: **which software is installed** (and which version), and **which environment variables are set**. Making both explicit is the first step towards reproducible systems, and later towards containers, which package both."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Packages, PATH and environment variables","md":"**Packages** are software prepared for your distribution, installed with a **package manager**. On Ubuntu that is `apt`: it downloads packages from repositories, resolves dependencies, verifies signatures, and can update or remove them cleanly. Never download random binaries when a package exists.\n\nWhen you type `jq`, the shell searches the directories listed in the **PATH** variable, in order, for a file called `jq`. `which jq` shows which one it found. If none: `command not found`.\n\n**Environment variables** are key=value pairs every process inherits from its parent:\n\n| Variable | Typical use |\n|---|---|\n| `PATH` | where to look for commands |\n| `HOME`, `USER` | who and where you are |\n| `LANG` | language and character encoding |\n| `API_URL`, `DATABASE_URL`, `PORT` | configuration for your apps |\n\nApps read their configuration from the environment: the **Twelve-Factor** principle \"store config in the environment\" lets the same code run in development, staging and production with different settings.\n\n- `NAME=value` sets a shell variable, visible only in this shell.\n- `export NAME=value` makes it part of the environment, so programs you start inherit it.\n- `NAME=value command` sets it for that one command only.\n- Put permanent settings in `~/.bashrc` (for your interactive shells), then `source ~/.bashrc` to reload it now."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Installing and configuring","md":"```term\n$ sudo apt update                 # refresh the list of available packages\n$ sudo apt install -y jq tree     # install (-y: don't ask)\n$ apt list --installed | grep jq  # is it installed?\n$ which jq                        # where does the command come from?\n$ echo $PATH                      # the search path\n$ env | sort                      # the whole environment\n$ export API_URL=https://api.lumen.example.com\n$ PORT=8080 ./server.sh           # one-off setting for one command\n$ echo 'export PATH=\"$HOME/bin:$PATH\"' >> ~/.bashrc\n$ source ~/.bashrc                # apply it to the current shell\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"The kitchen and the order slip","md":"Installed packages are the equipment in the kitchen: no blender, no smoothies, no matter how good the recipe. `PATH` is the list of cupboards the cook checks, in order, when the recipe says \"get the blender\". Environment variables are the order slip that comes with each dish: same recipe, but table 4 wants it without nuts and table 7 wants it extra spicy."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Making environments reproducible","md":"- **Write down dependencies**: a setup script, a package list, later a Dockerfile. Nothing should live only in someone's head or `.bashrc`.\n- **Same config mechanism everywhere**: environment variables (or files generated from them) in dev, CI and production.\n- **Pin versions** where behaviour matters, and update deliberately.\n- **Keep secrets out of `.bashrc`** and out of git; use a secret store (Security realm)."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Environment traps","md":"**`sudo` resets the environment.** `sudo mycommand` may not see your exported variables or your custom PATH. Use `sudo -E` carefully, or pass what is needed explicitly.\n\n**Editing `.bashrc` and forgetting to reload.** Changes apply to new shells, or after `source ~/.bashrc`.\n\n**Overwriting PATH.** `export PATH=$HOME/bin` (without `:$PATH`) removes every other directory; suddenly `ls` is \"not found\".\n\n**Unquoted variables.** `cd $DIR` breaks if `DIR` contains a space. Quote: `cd \"$DIR\"`."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Make the script work","md":"A teammate's health-check script fails on your laptop. Install what is missing, configure the environment it expects, and make your own scripts folder part of your PATH."}},"linux/pipes-text":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Who is hammering the login page?","md":"At 11:42 the login page slowed to a crawl. The access log had 1.2 million lines. Priya did not open it in an editor; she typed one line:\n\n```term\n$ grep 'POST /login' access.log | awk '{print $1}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -5\n```\n\nFour seconds later she had the answer: one IP address had sent 380,000 login attempts in an hour. A credential-stuffing attack. She blocked it at the firewall, and the page recovered.\n\nThat one line is five small programs, each doing one simple job, connected by pipes. Learning to build lines like that is one of the highest-value skills in operations."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Streams, pipes and redirects","md":"Every command has three **streams**:\n\n- **stdin** (0): input, from the keyboard or a pipe.\n- **stdout** (1): normal output.\n- **stderr** (2): errors and warnings.\n\n```diagram\npipes\n```\n\n| Syntax | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `a \\| b` | stdout of `a` becomes stdin of `b` |\n| `> file` | write stdout to a file (replace it) |\n| `>> file` | append stdout to a file |\n| `2> file` | write stderr to a file |\n| `> file 2>&1` | both stdout and stderr to the same file |\n| `2>/dev/null` | throw errors away |\n| `< file` | read stdin from a file |\n| `\\| tee file` | show output *and* save it |\n\nThe text toolbox:\n\n| Tool | Does |\n|---|---|\n| `grep` | keep lines matching a pattern (`-i` ignore case, `-v` invert, `-c` count, `-E` extended regex) |\n| `cut -d' ' -f1` | take fields by a delimiter |\n| `awk '{print $1, $9}'` | take columns, compute, filter (`$9 >= 500`) |\n| `sort` / `sort -n` / `sort -rn` | sort text / numbers / numbers descending |\n| `uniq -c` | collapse *adjacent* duplicates and count them (so sort first) |\n| `wc -l` | count lines |\n| `head` / `tail` | first / last lines |\n| `sed 's/old/new/g'` | replace text |\n| `tr a-z A-Z` | translate characters |"},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Log analysis recipes","md":"```term\n$ grep -c ' 500 ' access.log                         # how many 500 errors?\n$ awk '$9 >= 500' access.log | wc -l                  # the same, by status column\n$ awk '{print $1}' access.log | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head   # top IPs\n$ awk '{print $7}' access.log | sort -u | wc -l       # how many distinct paths\n$ grep -i error app.log | tail -n 20                  # the latest errors\n$ cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd                             # user names\n$ sed -i 's/timeout=5/timeout=30/' app.conf           # edit a file in place\n$ ./deploy.sh > deploy.log 2>&1                       # keep all output for later\n```\n\n```replay\ntitle: Building a pipeline step by step\nscenario: laptop\nsetup: printf '10.0.0.5 GET /\\n10.0.0.9 POST /login\\n10.0.0.5 POST /login\\n10.0.0.9 POST /login\\n10.0.0.9 POST /login\\n' > ~/mini.log\nstep: cat mini.log | Start by looking at the raw data.\nstep: grep login mini.log | Keep only the lines about /login.\nstep: grep login mini.log | awk '{print $1}' | Keep only the first column: the client IP.\nstep: grep login mini.log | awk '{print $1}' | sort | Sort, so identical IPs sit next to each other...\nstep: grep login mini.log | awk '{print $1}' | sort | uniq -c | ...because uniq -c only merges adjacent duplicates.\nstep: grep login mini.log | awk '{print $1}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | Biggest count first: 10.0.0.9 is the noisy one.\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"An assembly line","md":"A pipeline is a factory line. Raw material (the log) enters at one end. The first station throws away the parts you do not need (`grep`), the next trims each part to size (`awk`), the next sorts them into order (`sort`), the next counts identical parts (`uniq -c`), and the last puts the biggest piles at the front (`sort -rn`). Each station is simple. The line is powerful."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Turning one-liners into knowledge","md":"- **Build pipelines incrementally**: add one stage at a time and look at the output.\n- **Save good ones** in the team runbook or as small scripts. Today's clever one-liner is tomorrow's on-call tool.\n- **Logs at scale** live in log platforms (you will meet them in the Observability realm), but the thinking is the same: filter, extract, group, count, sort."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Pipeline traps","md":"**`uniq` without `sort`.** It only merges *adjacent* lines, so unsorted input gives wrong counts.\n\n**`> file` when you meant `>>`.** One character, and yesterday's log is gone.\n\n**`sort` sorts text by default.** `10` comes before `9`. Use `sort -n` for numbers.\n\n**Redirecting into the file you read.** `sort data.txt > data.txt` empties the file before `sort` reads it. Write to a new file, then move it.\n\n**Errors vanish into pipes.** Only stdout flows through `|`; errors still print on the screen (or nowhere, if you redirected them)."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Investigate the access log","md":"The login page was slow this morning. You have the web server's access log. Answer the team's questions with pipelines, and save a short report."}},"linux/processes":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The report that ate the server","md":"Every night at 2 a.m., a reporting script ran on Lumen's admin server. One night a change made it loop forever, pinning both CPUs at 100%. The admin site slowed to a crawl, and the on-call engineer got paged at 2:20.\n\nShe logged in, ran `top`, and saw it at once: `report.py` using 199% CPU for 18 minutes. She tried `kill` with its process ID; the script ignored the polite request because it was stuck in a tight loop without checking signals. `kill -9` ended it instantly. Then, instead of going back to sleep, she disabled the cron job so it would not start again at 2 a.m. tomorrow, and left a note for the team.\n\nFinding a process, understanding what it is doing, and stopping it safely are daily operations skills."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Processes, signals and exit codes","md":"A **process** is a running program. It has:\n\n- a **PID** (process ID) and a **parent** (PPID): processes form a tree, with `systemd` (PID 1) at the root;\n- an **owner** (the user it runs as), which limits what it can touch;\n- **resources**: CPU time, memory, open files, network sockets.\n\nYou talk to processes with **signals**:\n\n```diagram\nsignals\n```\n\nWhen a process ends, it returns an **exit code**: `0` means success, anything else means some kind of failure. The shell keeps the last one in `$?`, and `&&`, `||` and scripts make decisions with it.\n\n**Foreground and background**: a command normally runs in the foreground and holds your terminal. Add `&` to run it in the background, `jobs` lists background jobs, `nohup` keeps one running after you log out. (For real services you will use systemd instead, in the Services realm.)\n\n**Load average** (from `uptime` or `top`) is how many processes, on average, wanted CPU over the last 1, 5 and 15 minutes. Compare it with the number of CPUs (`nproc`): a load of 2.0 on 2 CPUs is busy; on 16 CPUs it is idle."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Watching and controlling processes","md":"```term\n$ ps aux                          # every process: user, PID, %CPU, %MEM, command\n$ ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head -5   # the five hungriest\n$ pgrep -a report                 # find PIDs by name\n$ top                             # live view (q quits); htop is friendlier\n$ uptime                          # load average\n$ kill 4312                       # SIGTERM: please stop\n$ kill -9 4312                    # SIGKILL: stop now (last resort)\n$ pkill -f report.py              # by name / command line\n$ sleep 300 &                     # run in the background\n$ jobs                            # background jobs in this shell\n$ echo $?                         # exit code of the last command\n$ false; echo $?                  # 1\n$ ls /nope && echo found || echo missing\n```\n\n```replay\ntitle: Exit codes drive decisions\nscenario: laptop\nstep: ls /etc/hostname; echo \"exit code: $?\" | Success: exit code 0.\nstep: ls /nope; echo \"exit code: $?\" | Failure: a non-zero exit code (2 for ls).\nstep: ls /etc/hostname && echo \"it exists\" | && runs the next command only after a success.\nstep: ls /nope 2>/dev/null || echo \"not there\" | || runs the next command only after a failure.\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A busy kitchen","md":"Processes are cooks. Each has a name tag (PID), works for a department (user) and uses burners and counter space (CPU and memory). `ps` is the kitchen roster, `top` is looking around the room to see who is sweating. `kill` (TERM) is asking a cook to finish the current plate and leave; `kill -9` is security escorting them out mid-sauce, burner still on. The exit code is the cook's note on the way out: \"0, all done\" or \"1, something went wrong\"."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Process hygiene","md":"- **Measure before you kill**: note the PID, the user, the command line and how long it has run. That is your evidence for the follow-up.\n- **TERM first, KILL last.** Give the process a chance to clean up (close files, finish requests, release locks).\n- **Fix the cause.** Killing a runaway process is mitigation; the cron job, deploy or bug that started it is the real fix.\n- **Long-running things belong in systemd**, not in a forgotten `nohup` in someone's shell."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Process traps","md":"**Killing the wrong PID.** PIDs are reused. Re-check with `ps -p PID -o pid,user,cmd` right before you kill.\n\n**`kill -9` as a habit.** Databases and queues can corrupt data or lose messages when killed without cleanup.\n\n**`grep` finds itself.** `ps aux | grep report` lists the grep process too. Use `pgrep -a report`.\n\n**Load average without context.** A number means nothing without the CPU count."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"The runaway job","md":"Your laptop's fans are screaming and everything is slow. Find out what is eating the CPU, stop it properly, and practise background jobs and exit codes."}},"linux/shell-basics":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The server with no screen","md":"Nadia's first production incident at Lumen started with a message: \"web3 is slow, can you have a look?\" She opened the cloud console expecting a dashboard with buttons. Instead she got a black window with a blinking cursor: `ubuntu@web3:~$`.\n\nServers have no desktop, no mouse and no file explorer. Almost every server on the internet runs Linux, and you drive it by typing. That felt hostile for about a week. Then Nadia noticed she could do in one line what took twenty clicks, repeat it on fifty servers with a loop, and paste it into a runbook so anyone could do it at 3 a.m.\n\nThe terminal is not old-fashioned. It is the most precise, scriptable and shareable interface operations work has."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Shell, prompt, filesystem","md":"The **shell** (here: `bash`) is the program that reads what you type, runs it and shows the result. The **prompt** tells you where you are:\n\n```term\ndev@laptop:~/projects$\n```\n\n`dev` is the user, `laptop` the machine, `~/projects` the current directory (`~` means your home folder, `/home/dev`), and `$` means a normal user (`#` means root).\n\nEverything lives in one tree that starts at `/`, the **root**:\n\n```diagram\nfs-tree\n```\n\nA **path** names a place in that tree:\n\n- **Absolute** paths start at `/`: `/var/log/nginx/error.log` works from anywhere.\n- **Relative** paths start where you are: if you are in `/var/log`, then `nginx/error.log` is the same file.\n- `.` is the current directory, `..` is its parent, `~` is your home.\n\nA command line has a shape: `command -options arguments`, for example `ls -l /etc`. Options change *how* a command behaves; arguments say *what* it works on."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Moving around","md":"```term\n$ pwd                 # where am I?\n$ ls                  # what is here?\n$ ls -l               # long format: permissions, owner, size, date\n$ ls -la /etc         # everything in /etc, hidden files (starting with .) too\n$ cd /var/log         # go to an absolute path\n$ cd nginx            # go into a subfolder (relative path)\n$ cd ..               # up one level\n$ cd -                # back to the previous directory\n$ cd                  # home (same as cd ~)\n$ ls --help           # every command explains itself\n$ history             # what you typed before\n```\n\nTwo keys save hours: **Tab** completes commands and file names (press it twice to see the options), and **↑** brings back previous commands.\n\n```replay\ntitle: Walking the tree\nscenario: laptop\nstep: pwd | You start in your home folder, /home/dev.\nstep: cd /var/log | An absolute path: it works from anywhere.\nstep: pwd | The prompt and pwd agree: you are in /var/log.\nstep: cd .. | Two dots: the parent directory.\nstep: ls | cd .. took you from /var/log up to /var; ls shows what is in it.\nstep: cd ~ | Tilde: straight back home.\nstep: ls -la | -a shows hidden files (names starting with a dot), like .bashrc.\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A building with corridors","md":"The filesystem is a building. `/` is the lobby, directories are corridors and rooms, files are the documents on the desks. An **absolute path** is a full address from the lobby: \"ground floor, east corridor, room 12\". A **relative path** is directions from where you stand: \"next door, then the second room\". `pwd` is checking the room number on the door; `cd ..` is stepping back into the corridor."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Habits of people who live in the terminal","md":"- **Look before you leap**: run `pwd` and `ls` before any command that changes things.\n- **Use Tab** constantly. It prevents typos and tells you whether a path exists.\n- **Prefer absolute paths in scripts and runbooks**: they work no matter where the reader is.\n- **Read `--help` and `man`** before asking: most answers are there, and they match the version you actually have."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Shell traps","md":"**Spaces in names.** `cd My Documents` tries to go to `My`. Quote it: `cd \"My Documents\"`, or press Tab.\n\n**Case matters.** `Readme.md` and `README.md` are different files on Linux.\n\n**Hidden files.** Configuration often lives in dot files (`.bashrc`, `.ssh/`). Plain `ls` does not show them; `ls -a` does.\n\n**The wrong machine.** Always read the prompt. `dev@laptop` and `ubuntu@prod-db-1` look similar at 3 a.m."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Find your way around","md":"Your laptop's filesystem looks like any Linux server's. Explore it: the system folders, the logs, your home folder and its hidden configuration."}},"linux/users-permissions":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"chmod 777, the universal \"fix\"","md":"Lumen's image upload broke after a deploy: `EACCES: permission denied, open '/srv/uploads/…'`. A tired engineer ran `sudo chmod -R 777 /srv` and uploads worked again. Ticket closed.\n\nThree months later, a vulnerability in an image library let an attacker write a file through the upload form. Normally that would have failed: the web app should only have been able to write into `/srv/uploads`. Because everything under `/srv` was writable by everyone, the attacker overwrote the app's own code. A one-line \"fix\" became a full compromise.\n\nPermissions are not an obstacle to work around. They are the walls between the parts of your system, and they decide how far an attacker or a bug can spread."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Users, groups and permission bits","md":"Every process runs as a **user**, and every file has an **owner** and a **group**. Users can belong to several groups. `root` (uid 0) is the administrator and bypasses permission checks.\n\nEach file carries three sets of three permissions:\n\n```diagram\npermissions\n```\n\n| Bit | On a file | On a directory |\n|---|---|---|\n| `r` read (4) | read its content | list the names inside |\n| `w` write (2) | change its content | create, rename, delete files inside |\n| `x` execute (1) | run it as a program | enter it (`cd`) and reach anything inside |\n\nLinux checks **owner** first, then **group**, then **others**: the first category that applies to you decides.\n\nNumbers add up per category: `7` = rwx, `6` = rw-, `5` = r-x, `4` = r--, `0` = ---. So:\n\n- `644` (rw-r--r--): normal files: owner edits, everyone reads.\n- `600` (rw-------): secrets, private keys: only the owner.\n- `755` (rwxr-xr-x): programs and directories everyone may use.\n- `750` (rwxr-x---): owner full, group can read/enter, others nothing.\n\n**sudo** runs a single command as root, if your user is allowed to (members of the `sudo` group on Ubuntu). It is safer than logging in as root: you only become root for one command, and it is logged."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Managing access","md":"```term\n$ whoami                                # who am I?\n$ id                                    # my uid, groups\n$ ls -l config.yml                      # read the permissions\n$ chmod 600 secrets.env                 # numbers…\n$ chmod u+x deploy.sh                   # …or symbols: u/g/o/a, +/-/=, r/w/x\n$ sudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash deploy   # new user with a home folder\n$ sudo groupadd webteam                 # new group\n$ sudo usermod -aG webteam deploy       # add deploy to webteam (-a: append!)\n$ sudo chown deploy:webteam /srv/site   # change owner and group\n$ sudo chown -R deploy:webteam /srv/site  # recursively\n$ sudo -u deploy cat /srv/site/index.html # test access as another user\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"An office building with badges","md":"Every employee has a badge (user) and belongs to departments (groups). Each room has a sign: what the owner may do, what the department may do, what visitors may do. The facilities manager (root) has a master key. `sudo` is borrowing the master key for one door, while security writes down who borrowed it. `chmod 777` is propping every door open with a chair."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Least privilege in practice","md":"- **Every service gets its own user** (`www-data`, `postgres`, `lumen-api`), owning only what it needs.\n- **Secrets are `600`** and owned by the service user that reads them.\n- **Humans use their own accounts** with sudo, never a shared root password. Logs then show who did what.\n- **Group-based access** (`webteam` can deploy to `/srv/site`) scales better than adding individuals to files."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Permission traps","md":"**`chmod 777` as a fix.** It works by removing all protection. Find out *which* user needs *which* access, and grant exactly that.\n\n**`usermod -G` without `-a`.** It *replaces* all of the user's groups. You can remove someone (or yourself) from `sudo` by accident.\n\n**Forgetting directories need `x`.** A file can be `644`, but if a parent directory is `700` and owned by someone else, nobody else can reach it.\n\n**Group changes need a new login.** After `usermod -aG docker you`, your current shell still has the old groups. Log out and back in.\n\n**`sudo` with redirects.** `sudo echo x > /etc/file` fails: the redirect is done by *your* shell, not by sudo. Use `echo x | sudo tee /etc/file`."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Set up a team folder","md":"The web team needs a shared folder for the site, a deploy user, and a secrets file that only that user can read. Set it up with the least privilege that works, and prove it."}}};
